# Peers

Public repos worth tailing for Effect, Alchemy, and XState patterns. Discovery uses `pnpm find-peers` and the [find-peers skill](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/skills/find-peers/SKILL.md). Shared lines do not mean identical versions. Drift is expected.

S means an upstream reference or an adopted pattern; A means active, close to our stack, and studied; B means relevant and worth tailing. C means screened without a relevant pattern for current work; D means off-stack; E means stale without a useful reference; F means no usable reference remains. The skill defines the full rubric. Rank measures usefulness. Popularity and version parity do not determine it.

The full dataset lives in [peers.json](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/.brain/data/peers.json). The content build filters and sorts those rows; changing a tier never deletes a peer. The main roster shows S through B. C through F stay in **Also seen**.

## Our pins

The build reads pins from rat-stack's root, infra, and core manifests. `=` means a peer's recorded version matches our current pin; `≠` means it differs. We do not infer compatibility from either marker.

<PeerPins />

Each peer has a checked date and a default-branch commit linked below. `—` means the inspected project manifests have no direct declaration. A transitive dependency may still exist. Catalogs resolve to their declared versions; ranges are labeled and lockfile resolutions are shown where available. Vendored source mirrors and historical test fixtures do not count as current project pins. Targeted discovery also checks XState 6 alpha and `@xstate/effect` independently of Alchemy; single-line peers qualify when their machine or runtime seam is relevant.

Refresh peers, versions, and tiers every week or two and after every shared-line bump. The repo-native gardener pass owns the refresh. Update the dataset. The build generates this page's table rows.

